# Architecture

Next.js 15 App Router + TypeScript (strict). Follow the existing structure; do not invent a second architecture alongside it. Full map: `docs/03-architecture.md` and `docs/architecture/`.

## Layers (dependencies point inward only)

| Layer | Folder | Responsibility |
|-------|--------|----------------|
| UI | `src/app/(app)`, `src/components` | Presentation, local UI state, accessibility, loading/empty/error states |
| API routes | `src/app/api` | Validate request + origin, call one service, respond/redirect. No business logic |
| Services | `src/services` | Use-cases; return **display-ready** models (formatted strings, chart numbers) |
| Domain | `src/domain` | Financial formulas, insight rules. Pure functions, no I/O, fully tested |
| Integration adapters | `src/integrations` | Marketplace HTTP/OAuth/payload mapping behind the `MarketplaceAdapter` port |
| Repositories | `src/repositories` | Database access through the `IntegrationRepository` interface; always workspace-scoped |
| Shared lib | `src/lib` | Dates (IST day keys), URL params, CSV, env, crypto, db client |
| Internal docs portal | `src/internal`, `src/app/internal` | Renders docs/rules for the team; never imported by product code |

- UI must not contain authoritative financial calculations; pages never add or subtract money.
- All analytics read through `services/ledger.ts` so every page and report reconciles.
- Repositories must not call UI or embed fee/profit formulas; services must not import Prisma directly.
- Adapters must not touch the database; services talk to providers only through the port.

## Contracts and validation

- Typed service and repository interfaces with explicit inputs/outputs.
- Zod at every trust boundary: URL params (`lib/search-params.ts`), env (`lib/env.ts`), provider responses (`integrations/*/schemas.ts`), forms and CSV rows.
- Errors shown to users are allow-listed messages, never raw exception or provider text.

## Structure guidelines

- URL search params are the state for filters, sorting and pagination; server components by default, client components only for interactivity (charts, filters, toggles).
- Reuse shared UI primitives in `src/components/ui`; feature components live in their feature folder.
- Avoid oversized components; split when a file mixes fetching, domain math and dense presentation.
- No generic abstractions without a second real use. No circular dependencies.
- Bounded work in serverless handlers (page/time budgets) and resumable state for long jobs.

## Example boundary

```typescript
// ✅ UI calls a service; service uses ledger + domain
const model = getDashboardModel(filters);

// ❌ UI computes contribution profit with raw JS numbers
const profit = order.total - order.cogs - order.fees;
```
